/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - The inductor starts from L, the average current Iavg and the peak-to-peak ripple ΔIL.
   - Ipk = Iavg + ΔIL/2
   - Ipk is the peak winding current in A; Iavg the average current in A; ΔIL the peak-to-peak ripple in A.
   - N = L · Ipk / (Bmax · Ae)
   - N is the number of turns; L the inductance in H; Ipk the peak current in A; Bmax the allowed peak flux density in T (about 0.3 T for ferrite); Ae the effective core area in m² from the datasheet.
   - Round N up to a whole number, then recompute B = L·Ipk/(N·Ae) to confirm B stays below Bmax.
   - ℓg = µ0 · N² · Ae / L
   - ℓg is the total air-gap length in m (valid when the gap dominates core reluctance); µ0 = 4π × 10−7 H/m; N the turns; Ae in m²; L in H.
   - Most energy sits in the air gap, so the gap sets both L and the saturation current.
   - Wire comes from a current density J of 2 to 4 A/mm²: copper area Acu = Irms/J, where Irms is the RMS winding current.
*/
