/* book Worked example: Q: Design the inductor for the worked example of Buck converter: Vs = 50 V, Vo = 20 V, Io = 4 A, fs = 100 kHz, L = 100 µH, ripple ΔIL = 1.2 A. The core has Ae = 1.0 cm² = 1.0 × 10−4 m² and Bmax = 0.3 T. (a) Peak current. (b) Turns and resulting flux. (c) Air gap. (d) Copper area and wire diameter for J = 4 A/mm².
   1. Peak current: Ipk = Io + ΔIL/2 = 4 + 0.6 = 4.6 A. The core must carry 4.6 A, not 4 A.
   2. Turns before rounding: N = L·Ipk/(Bmax·Ae) = (100 × 10−6 × 4.6)/(0.3 × 1.0 × 10−4) = 4.6 × 10−4 / 3.0 × 10−5 = 15.33.
   3. Round up to 16 turns. Check: B = 4.6 × 10−4/(16 × 1.0 × 10−4) = 0.2875 ≈ 0.29 T, below 0.3 T.
   4. Gap: ℓg = µ0N²Ae/L = (4π × 10−7 × 256 × 1.0 × 10−4)/(100 × 10−6) = 3.22 × 10−4 m = 0.32 mm.
   5. Copper area: the ripple adds little, Irms = √(4² + 1.2²/12) = 4.01 A, so Acu = Irms/J ≈ 4 A / 4 A/mm² = 1.0 mm².
   6. Single-wire diameter: d = √(4A/π) = √(4 × 1.0/π) = 1.13 ≈ 1.1 mm, above twice the 0.21 mm skin depth. Use about eight strands of 0.40 mm (8 × π/4 × 0.40² = 1.0 mm²), or litz wire of the same copper area.
   Answer: Ipk = 4.6 A; 16 turns give B = 0.29 T; gap 0.32 mm; copper area 1.0 mm² (1.1 mm single wire, split into strands under 0.4 mm for 100 kHz). */
