/* book exam traps: Sizing the core on average current instead of peak. The core saturates at the top of every ripple, inductance collapses and switch current spikes. | Choosing C from ΔIL/(8fsC) alone. Ripple is usually set by ESR × ΔIL, so the part meets the value on paper and fails on the bench. | Forgetting that the air gap stores energy. An ungapped ferrite core has high inductance but saturates at small current, so the design works at no load and fails at full load. | Using DC resistance for loss at 100 kHz. Skin and proximity effect can make Rac several times Rdc, so the winding runs hotter than calculated. | Mixing units: Ae in cm² (1 cm² = 1 × 10−4 m²) and L in µH. Convert both to m² and H before dividing. */
